[[build-streaming]]
=== Streaming and memory mapping

[abstract]
A *stream* is a list too large or too long-lived to hold in memory at once,
kept in a file and read or written in batches. Any pool can read a stream
another pool is writing, and a finished stream file can be read at random
without loading it. This section specifies the stream file format, its
compression, how processes share an open stream, how every write reaches
the file through a single writer in the nexus, and what a reader may rely
on while the stream is still being written.

**Stream types.** Three Morloc types name a stream, each a 16-byte slot in
voidstar (schema codes `F`, `I`, `O`):

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Type | Use
| `IFile [a]` | A finished file, read at random by element index.
| `IStream a` | A stream read forward in batches. Several readers share one cursor, so together they consume each batch once.
| `OStream a` | A stream written in batches.
|===

The slot's first byte says what follows in its last eight: 0 for a relative
pointer to a path (length, then bytes), 1 for the index of an open stream in
the run's stream registry (below). A path can cross between runs; an index
means something only inside the run that opened it.

**File format.** A stream file is a sequence of packets (<<build-packets>>):

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Part | Contents
| Head | A stream packet (type 3) whose metadata holds the schema of the list, `a` followed by the element schema. Its length field is all ones.
| Batches | Data packets, one per batch. Each payload is a self-contained voidstar array of that batch's elements, its pointers relative to the start of the payload.
| Footer | A footer packet (type 4) whose metadata holds a batch index (offset and element count of every batch), a final marker, and a status.
| Tail | 8 bytes: the footer's length as a 32-bit integer, then the magic bytes reversed (`07 07 f8 6d`).
|===

A reader finds the footer from the end of the file through the tail, so
random access needs no scan. The status is `closed` for a stream written to
the end, `paused` for one a writer stopped and may resume, or `failed` for
one whose writer died in the middle of an update. While a stream is open for
writing, a temporary footer carrying only diagnostics is rewritten after
every batch, so the file always ends in a valid tail.

**Compression.** Each batch is compressed separately with Zstandard, so a
reader can decompress any batch without the others. A batch is split into
independent frames of at most 16 MiB, listed in the batch's metadata, so
they can be decompressed in parallel straight into shared memory. The user's
compression level 0-9 maps to Zstandard levels 0, 1, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 19, 21
and 22, with long-distance matching from level 7.

**Sharing an open stream.** A stream's identity lives in a *registry* in
shared memory, created with the run's first volume. Each slot records the
file's path, schema and element count, a write buffer, and a generation
number that changes when the slot is opened and when it is released. A
stream index carries the generation it was made with, and every operation
checks it, so an index to a closed stream can never touch the stream that
later reuses its slot. A process reading a stream keeps its own descriptor
and read-only mapping of the file.

**Writing a stream.** Every output stream has exactly one writer: a thread
in the nexus, started when the stream is opened. No pool opens, locks or
writes the file of a stream it writes to; it only hands the nexus its
elements. Standard output and standard error are written the same way.

[mermaid]
....
flowchart LR
    A[pool A] -->|append elements| B
    P[pool B] -->|append elements| B
    C[forked child] -->|append elements| B
    subgraph SHM[shared memory]
      B[stream's write buffer<br/>under the stream's lock]
      Q[(queue of full buffers,<br/>flushes and the close)]
    end
    B -->|full buffer, flush, close| Q
    Q --> W[writer in the nexus:<br/>compress, write, footer]
    W --> F[stream file, or<br/>standard output]
....

. `@open` and `@append` ask the nexus to open the file. It takes an
  exclusive lock on the file, writes the head (or, for `@append`, finds the
  end of the last complete batch), registers the stream and starts its
  writer.
. A `@write` from any process appends its elements to the stream's buffer
  in shared memory. A {cpp}, Python or R pool writing to a file or to
  standard output or error encodes the batch straight into the buffer,
  inside one transaction on the stream's lock that it commits or aborts
  whole; an element that is a string or a byte or number list is copied and
  checked in one pass, and in {cpp} so is a list of them. Otherwise the batch
  is encoded first and the lock is held only for the copy. One `@write`'s elements stay together; writes from different
  processes interleave only between writes, in the order they took the
  lock.
. A full buffer is moved onto the stream's queue and replaced by an empty
  one, still under the lock and without touching the file. The writer takes
  buffers off the queue in order, compresses them on several threads (as
  under Compression above), and appends them to the file in
  queue order, so compression never reorders elements.
. The queue holds a bounded number of buffers (for a compressed stream, the
  number of compression threads clamped to 2-4, at most 7, and lowered as
  more streams are written; 1 for an uncompressed one). A writer that finds it full waits for the nexus,
  never for another pool, so streams add no way for pools to deadlock.
. `@flush` queues the buffered elements and returns once the nexus has
  written them, so a reader opened afterwards sees them. `@close` does the
  same and also waits for the final footer, so when it returns the file is
  complete, its lock is released and its path can be opened again at once.

A process that exits or dies after its `@write` returned loses nothing: its
elements are already in shared memory, and the nexus writes them when the
stream is flushed or closed. A process that dies while holding the stream's
lock may leave the buffer half updated; the stream is then failed, the
batches queued before it are still written, and the footer says `failed`. A
stream the program never closes is discarded when its index is dropped or
the run ends: what was queued is written, the unflushed elements are
dropped, and the file keeps its temporary footer, the sign that its writer
did not finish. A daemon recovering from a pool crash (<<eco-serving>>)
stops every writer before it discards shared memory, and each file ends
with a `failed` status.

**Readers.** An `IFile` maps the file and locates an element through the
batch index. A batch stored uncompressed and 8-byte aligned is read where it
lies in the mapping; any other batch is decompressed or copied into shared
memory first. An `IStream` reads forward from the shared cursor. A reader
that opens a stream still being written sees the batches written before it
opened, up to the temporary footer; reading past the end yields an empty
batch. Reading a compressed stream forward, the runtime decodes the next
batches ahead of the reader on at most two threads, so a reader costs about
the CPU of one decode however many cores the machine has.

**Rewriting a file.** Opening an `OStream` on a file that already has data
writes a new file and renames it into place, so a reader that still has the
old file mapped keeps a consistent view. Where a rename is impossible (the
directory is not writable, say) and nothing in the program is reading the
file, it is truncated in place instead. The runtime never deletes a stream
file; only a half-written temporary file is removed on failure.

